               <p xml:id="idm252">Hartmann wrote movements 1, 2 and 4 in 1855. At that time the German pianist and conductor Hans von Bülow probably heard the work during his visit to Copenhagen. When Hartmann decided to publish the sonata thirty years later, he added the third movement, Passions-Intermezzo, and dedicated the work to Bülow. The latter was delighted to accept the dedication of what he called "eines Ihrer schönen Musterwerke". He called Hartmann "Princeps der skandinavischen Tonkünstler".</p>
            </history>
